---
export const prerender = true
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { PLUGIN_REPO_URL } from '#utils/site-config'
const pageTitle = 'About'

const facts = [
  'It runs in Claude Code: the terminal, the IDE extensions and the Code tab of the desktop app.',
  'It costs one haiku request on the first prompt of each session. Refining a prompt adds one haiku request (two if you answer its questions), and refining from the first-prompt dialog adds one more classification when you send the rewrite.',
  'It does not store your prompts. It saves two things across sessions: your Goal, Workflow and Scope picks in the refine band, and a log of up to 200 entries recording each rating, the models, the option you chose, the date, the session cost, turns, context and effort. /triage-stats reads that log. It writes no files and makes no network requests of its own.',
  'All of its logic, fifteen hooks and two commands, is in hooks/register.ts, with tests you run using claude plugin test .',
  'It is released under the MIT License.',
]
---

<Layout
  pageTitle={pageTitle}
  pageDescription="What the prompt-triage Claude Code plugin does, what it costs and where it runs."
>
  <h2>{pageTitle}</h2>
  <h3>... prompt-triage, a Claude Code plugin</h3>

  <p>
    prompt-triage is a Claude Code plugin maintained by Shawn Sandy. It looks at the first prompt of
    each new session, rates how complex it is, offers to switch models before the work starts, and
    raises the effort level when it is too low for the task.
  </p>

  <p>
    It can also review a prompt before you send it and put an improved version in the prompt box,
    from the first-prompt dialog, the /refine-prompt command or the refine band above the prompt
    input. In the Claude desktop app it can hand a complex or long-running task, or any prompt you
    pick, to a new session, by asking Claude to start one.
  </p>
  <p>A few facts:</p>
  <ul>
    {facts.map(fact => <li>{fact}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    Read <a href="/content">how it works</a>, or go to the <a href={PLUGIN_REPO_URL}
      >GitHub repository</a
    > for the source, the full README and the issue tracker. This site is built with Astro.
  </p>
</Layout>
